The 2026 QS World University Rankings by Subject for Data Science and Artificial Intelligence places Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) at rank 1, with 201 institutions from 37 countries and regions appearing in the global table as of the April 3, 2026 data cut. A median rank cannot be computed for the full list: the institution at the median position has only a published rank range (101-200), and rank ranges are not used for medians.

## How the 201 ranked programs are distributed across countries

The United States holds the largest share of the list with 41 ranked institutions, more than double the next country. The United Kingdom follows with 22, while China (Mainland) and Australia each contribute 13. India and Spain each have 8, and Canada, Italy, and Malaysia each place 7. Hong Kong SAR, China rounds out the top ten countries with 6.

This distribution tells a clear story about where the field's academic capacity concentrates. The US presence is not just large in absolute terms — it also spans the full range of the ranking, from the very top to the middle of the table. The UK's 22 programs make it the second-largest contributor, while the relatively high counts for Australia and Malaysia suggest that data science and AI education has become a significant export sector for these countries, not merely an add-on to existing computer science departments.

## What separates the top tier from the rest

The score bands reveal a striking discontinuity. Eleven institutions score 90 or above, and 24 more fall in the 80–89 range. That gives 35 programs with scores of 80 or higher. But the next band — 70–79 — contains only 15 institutions. In other words, the score distribution is not evenly spread: 35 institutions score 80 or higher, while the 70-79 band contains 15 institutions. These bands cover only the institutions whose overall score was published.

The rank bands confirm this pattern. The top 10 ranks are filled by 10 institutions, ranks 11–20 by another 10, and ranks 21–30 by 10 more. The 31–50 band holds 20 institutions, and ranks 51–100 contain 50. QS publishes a rank band of 101-200 for 101 institutions, out of 201 ranked programs in total; these institutions have no exact rank, so their positions are not subdivided into a 101-150 band. No institutions appear beyond rank 150; the list stops at 201 total entries.

The practical takeaway for prospective students: the top 30 programs are tightly clustered in score terms, but the drop from the top 50 into the 51–100 range is where the ranking becomes more about institutional breadth than about a narrow set of elite performers.

